  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Examination of Amphibian Pathogen Introduction and sperad in Hong Kong through the International Wildlife Trade</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Examination of Amphibian Pathogen Introduction and Spread in Hong Kong through the International Wildlife Trade. In partnership with EcoHealth Alliance, this project will support a conservation effort for the Hong Kong Cascade frog, by mitigating population level impacts of batrachochytrium dendrobatidis, the fungus responsible for the disease chytridiomycosis, using enhanced surveillance techniques and will influence amphibian trade policies at an international wildlife trade hub. Specific activities include: (1) conducting surveillance in Hong Kong to determine if batrachochytrium dendrobatidis is being introduced through the amphibian trade by swabbing the skin of the Chinese bullfrogs being imported from mainland China immediately upon arrival at Hong Kong International Airport; (2) collecting and filtering 25 freshwater samples for batrachochytrium dendrobatidis from Shek Pik Reservoir, Aberdeen Reservoir and Shing Mun Reservoir in Hong Kong; (3) recommending policy to traders and Hong Kong regulating agencies to mitigate further introduction of batrachochytrium dendrobatidis from captive amphibians in Hong Kong.</narrative>
  </description>
